Stop. You've spent 78 turns convincing yourself the data is inconsistent — it isn't, and that assumption has wasted everyone's time. The reference ledger was generated from PRODUCTION code that uses ordinary Python float arithmetic and Python's built-in round(), NOT Decimal. Two things you've been stubbornly doing are fighting the real semantics:

1. Decimal(str(unit_price)) "cleans up" the float (e.g. turns the binary double for 3.335 back into exactly 3.335). Production never saw a clean 3.335 — it saw the binary double, which differs in the 17th digit. That difference flips some half-way roundings. This is the entire source of your "discrepancy."
2. ROUND_HALF_UP is not what Python's round() does. Built-in round() uses banker's rounding (round-half-to-even) on the actual binary float. You'd have caught this if you'd checked instead of theorizing for 60 turns.

